# **1.  Introduction**

The design and simulation of a functional computer at the gate level is one of the most demanding and insightful exercises in a computer architecture curriculum. This project — the 23-Bit Mini Computer — was undertaken as part of the CSE-2114 Computer Architecture Laboratory course at KUET. The entire system was constructed inside Logisim 2.7.1, a free, educational, logic-circuit simulator, using only primitive gates as the fundamental building blocks.

The project is divided into two logically connected systems. The first is a hardware implementation of Booth's Signed Multiplication Algorithm, which operates on 23-bit 2's complement operands and produces a 46-bit signed product. The second is a simple yet complete accumulator-based CPU that embeds the Booth multiplier as a functional unit alongside a custom 23-bit ALU, a microprogram-based control unit, a register file, and a memory interface.

Working entirely at the gate level — from individual full-adder cells up through shift registers, multiplexers, ROMs, and the final processor datapath — gives a uniquely concrete understanding of how abstract computer architecture concepts translate into physical hardware. Every wire in this design carries deliberate meaning, and every sub-circuit exists to solve a specific problem in the datapath or control path.

## **1.1  Objectives**

- Design a 23-bit ALU supporting AND, OR, ADD, and SUB operations with full status flag generation.

- Implement Booth's signed multiplication algorithm as a sequential hardware circuit controlled by a microprogram ROM.

- Build an accumulator-based CPU integrating the ALU and multiplier, driven by an 8-instruction ISA.

- Verify all circuits through Logisim simulation, including edge cases for signed arithmetic.

- Document the complete design hierarchy, control word format, and instruction set in detail.

## **1.2  Scope**

The design covers gate-level implementation of arithmetic circuits, sequential controller design with microprogramming, instruction set architecture (ISA) definition, datapath construction, and multi-cycle instruction execution. It does not cover pipelining, caches, or I/O interfaces beyond the RAM block available in Logisim.

