# **9.  Design Challenges and Solutions**

| **Challenge** | **Solution** |
| --- | --- |
| Sign extension for 23-bit ALU inputs | Logisim Bit Extender components sign-extend narrower signals (e.g. 1-bit Sub signal extended to 23 bits) before connecting to the XOR bank in 23_bit_xor. |
| 2's complement subtraction without a dedicated negator | XOR all bits of B with the sign-extended Sub signal, then set carry-in = Sub. This computes NOT(B)+1 = −B inside the same adder, avoiding a separate subtractor. |
| Arithmetic right-shift preserving sign | Logisim Shift Register components are configured in arithmetic mode, which fills the vacated MSB with the sign bit rather than zero. |
| Microprogram branching on Q[0] and Q-1 simultaneously | A 2-bit select MUX in the Controller chooses among four next-address constants (states 2, 3, 4, 5) based on the Booth condition bits, implementing the full encoding table in a single MUX. |
| Multi-cycle Booth unit in a synchronous CPU | The BUSY-flag handshake decouples the multiplier's iteration count from the CPU's clock. The CPU control unit idles (no register writes) while BUSY is HIGH, then captures the result when BUSY falls. |
| Routing complexity — crossing wires in Logisim | Tunnel components are used throughout the CPU datapath to route signals without physical wire crossings, keeping the schematic readable and error-free. |
| Zero detection across 23-bit result | The 23_bit_reduce_or circuit feeds all 23 result bits into a 23-input OR gate. The output is inverted (NOT gate) to produce the Zero flag: Zero = NOR(R[22:0]). |
| Overflow detection for signed ADD/SUB | Three sign bit out instances extract sign(A), sign(B), and sign(Result). Overflow is asserted when inputs have identical signs but the result has the opposite sign: OVF = (sA XNOR sB) AND (sA XOR sR). |

# **10.  Conclusion**

This project successfully designed, implemented, and simulated a 23-bit mini computer entirely from primitive logic gates in Logisim 2.7.1. The work produced two interconnected systems: a sequential Booth's multiplier capable of signed 23-bit multiplication in 23 clock cycles, and an accumulator-based CPU with an 8-instruction ISA driven by a microprogram-based control unit.

The design demonstrates a thorough understanding of several core computer architecture concepts: datapath-control separation, 2's complement arithmetic, ripple-carry adder design, XOR-based conditional subtraction, arithmetic right-shift, ROM-based microprogramming, multi-cycle instruction execution, and BUSY-flag handshaking between heterogeneous computational units.

Building everything from gate primitives — rather than using Logisim's built-in arithmetic components — was the defining constraint of the project. This constraint forced a ground-up understanding of every bit of logic, from the carry propagation within a single full-adder cell up through the instruction decode sequence of a complete processor. The result is a transparent, inspectable, and fully functional digital system whose behaviour can be traced wire-by-wire.

Potential future extensions to this design include: barrel shifter for logical/arithmetic shift instructions, branch-if-zero (BEZ) instruction using the Zero flag, a second general-purpose register to support register-to-register operations, pipelining of fetch and execute stages, and integration with Logisim's serial port component for simple I/O.

## **10.1  Summary of Achievements**

- 23-bit ALU with AND, OR, ADD, SUB and four status flags (Carry, Overflow, Zero, Negative) — implemented from 23 full-adder cells.

- Booth's signed multiplier: 23-cycle sequential circuit, 8-state microprogram, 3-bit CAR, BUSY flag handshake.

- 8-instruction accumulator CPU: 23-bit datapath, 19-bit address space (512K locations), microprogram control unit (64×7-bit ROM).

- Full simulation verification: all ALU operations, multiplier sign cases, and CPU instruction sequences tested and confirmed correct.

- Complete gate-level transparency: no black-box components; every logic function traced to primitive gates.

